.md
# UI Elements
We provide a large set of basic UI elements used for creating buttons, sliders, text fields, and more.

Input elements such as `Button`, `InputRange`, `InputText`, `InputFile`, etc., can be found in the <small>GUI section</small> of the symbols reference documentation.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
# UI Elements
We provide a large set of basic UI elements used for creating buttons, sliders, text fields, and more.

Input elements such as `Button`, `InputRange`, `InputText`, `InputFile`, etc., can be found in the <small >GUI section</small> of the symbols reference documentation.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Each standard input element generates `EventObject`, to which you can assign a handler function. You don't necessarily need to assign it to a variable. For example (see InputButton):

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Each standard input element generates `EventObject`, to which you can assign a handler function. You don't necessarily need to assign it to a variable. For example (see InputButton):

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
EventHandler[InputButton["Click"], Beep]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
is equally valid:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
is equally valid:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
btn = InputButton["Click"];
EventHandler[btn, Beep];
btn

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
There is also a helper symbol `Button` that allows you to assign handlers implicitly:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
There is also a helper symbol `Button` that allows you to assign handlers implicitly:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Button["Click", Beep[]]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Here are some other examples with `InputRange`:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Here are some other examples with `InputRange`:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
EventHandler[InputRange[0, 1, 0.1], Function[value, radius = value]]
% // EventFire; (*BB[*)(* just to initialize radius symbol *)(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNhYGAo5gcSAUX5ZZkpqSn+BSWZ+XnFaYwgCS4g4Zyfm5uaV+KUXxEMUqxsbm6exgSSBPGCSnNSg9mAjOCSosy8dLBYSFFpKpoKkDkeqYkpEFXBILO1sCgJSczMQVYCAOFrJEU="*)(*]BB*)

Graphics[{LightBlue, Disk[], Pink, Disk[{0,0}, radius // Offload]}, ImageSize->200, "Controls"->False]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
:::tip
Apply EventFire to any EventObject to manually fire an event with a default value to initialize your variables, if needed.
:::

You can also add a label and an initial values as the 4th argument to an InputRange:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
:::tip
Apply EventFire to any EventObject to manually fire an event with a default value to initialize your variables, if needed.
:::

You can also add a label and an initial values as the 4th argument to an InputRange:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
InputRange[0, 1, 0.1, 0.7, "Label" -> "Radius"]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Here is an example using InputSelect:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Here is an example using InputSelect:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
angle = 45 Degree;
EventHandler[
    InputSelect[{Pi/2 -> "90", Pi/4 -> "45", 0 -> "0"}, Pi/4], 
    Function[value, angle = value]
]

Graphics[{Rotate[Rectangle[{0,0}, {1,1}], angle // Offload]}, "Controls"->False, ImageSize->200]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Here is a simple text input:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Here is a simple text input:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
text = "Example";
EventHandler[InputText[], Function[value, text = value]]
Graphics[Table[{
  Hue[i/10., 1., 1.], Rotate[Text[Style[text // Offload, FontSize -> RandomInteger[{12, 24}]], RandomReal[{-1,1}, 2]], RandomChoice[{Pi, Pi/4, Pi/2, 0}]]
}, {i, 10}]]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## Grouping
To group elements both visually and at the event level, use InputGroup:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## Grouping
To group elements both visually and at the event level, use InputGroup:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
slider = InputRange[0, 1, 0.1]; 
checkbox = InputCheckbox[];

n = EvaluationNotebook[];
joined = InputGroup[<|"Checkbox" -> checkbox, "Slider" -> slider|>, "Label" -> "Group"];
EventHandler[joined, NotebookWrite[n, #]&]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
It merges an association (as above) or a list of EventObjects into a new one. You do not need to assign separate EventHandlers - just __one joined handler__ is enough. 

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
It merges an association (as above) or a list of EventObjects into a new one. You do not need to assign separate EventHandlers - just __one joined handler__ is enough. 

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## Chaining Events
Most GUI elements support chaining, where each reuses the same EventObject. It’s passed as the first argument:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## Chaining Events
Most GUI elements support chaining, where each reuses the same EventObject. It’s passed as the first argument:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
ev = EventObject[];

InputButton[ev, "Topic" -> "Button"]
InputRange[ev, 0, 1, 0.1, "Topic" -> "Slider"]

EventHandler[ev, {
  "Button" -> Beep,
  "Slider" -> Print
}];

